**Automatic release conditions**

The page says the sentinel "can be released by the system, again if the battery power is too low or the document is not active or visible." It also says: "Only active documents can acquire screen wake locks and previously acquired locks are automatically released when document becomes inactive." Navigating away from the tab also releases the lock: the release-listener example updates the UI "if the wake lock has been released for any reason (such as navigating away from the active window/tab)."

Listen for the release event:

```js
wakeLock.addEventListener("release", () => {
  // the wake lock has been released
  statusElem.textContent = "Wake Lock has been released";
});
```

**Secure-context requirement**

> **Secure context:** This feature is available only in secure contexts (HTTPS), in some or all supporting browsers.

**Recommended re-acquire pattern**

The page recommends listening for `visibilitychange` and re-requesting the lock when the document becomes visible again:

```js
document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", async () => {
  if (wakeLock !== null && document.visibilityState === "visible") {
    wakeLock = await navigator.wakeLock.request("screen");
  }
});
```

The page also notes that a released sentinel "can no longer be used," so a new one must be requested. The example does not handle a rejected request, so you may want to wrap the call in try/catch.

**Browser compatibility summary**

The page is marked "Baseline 2025 – Newly available": "Since March 2025, this feature works across the latest devices and browser versions. This feature might not work in older devices or browsers."

The compatibility tables did not come through in the page content, so I can't confirm the minimum Safari iOS or Chrome Android versions. Check the "Browser compatibility" section on the live MDN page for those.

